TastingNotes:
Bought at La Maison des Millésimes in Paris. Tiny shop, tightly curated, and their niche is that they sell Bordeaux when it is ready to drink instead of when it is released. You walk out with a bottle someone else already waited thirty years on.
&#8232;
It showed. The fill level was perfect and the rim had barely started to brown. Whoever held this bottle held it properly.
&#8232;
The aroma was subtle. It never pushed, and I stopped waiting for it to open up and just took it for what it was. On the palate it was lighter than the Pomerol I am used to, more red-fruited and savory than dense, with the acidity carrying it.
&#8232;
The tannins were the finest I have ever had. Completely dissolved, nothing catching anywhere. Extraordinarily elegant start to finish.
&#8232;
Probably a little over the hill, like its consumer. I would not hold a bottle like this any longer than necessary, and I would only buy another one with provenance this clean.
